# ----------------------------------------------------------------------
# kenzed.in — static Next.js export, served by OpenLiteSpeed.
#
# This file used to be four times longer, and all of it was fighting one
# problem that no longer exists.
#
# Next's export USED to emit a page file and an RSC-payload directory with the
# same name — `technology.html` next to `technology/`. Both claim the URL
# `/technology`. LiteSpeed's built-in directory handler wanted to redirect it
# to `/technology/`; a rewrite here wanted to serve `technology.html`. Which
# one answered was not deterministic: on the live site, ten routes redirected
# to a directory with no index and returned 404 while four served correctly,
# and `/privacy` moved from one group to the other between two requests
# minutes apart. Ordering the rewrite first and terminating it with [END] did
# not settle it, because the directory handler resolves the request before the
# document-root rewrites are consulted at all.
#
# `trailingSlash: true` in next.config.ts removes the collision instead of
# arbitrating it: Next now emits `technology/index.html`, so there is exactly
# one thing at that path and the server's ordinary directory handling is
# correct without help. Every rule that existed to win that race is gone.
#
# Two notes carried forward, both still true:
#
#  1. `<IfModule>` is a trap here. LiteSpeed reports mod_rewrite but NOT
#     mod_dir, mod_headers, mod_deflate or mod_mime, so anything wrapped in
#     those guards is silently skipped — which is how this site once shipped
#     with no security headers at all. Directives LiteSpeed supports natively
#     are declared bare.
#  2. Response headers are NOT set here. `Header` is a mod_headers directive
#     and OpenLiteSpeed does not implement it in .htaccess, guarded or bare.
#     They live in the `context /` block of
#     /usr/local/lsws/conf/vhosts/kenzed.in/vhost.conf, which is the only
#     place LiteSpeed honours them. Keep the two in step: if this site ever
#     moves to real Apache, the headers have to come back here.
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------

Options -MultiViews -Indexes
DirectoryIndex index.html

RewriteEngine On

# Canonical host: strip www.
RewriteCond %{HTTP_HOST} ^www\.(.+)$ [NC]
RewriteRule ^ https://%1%{REQUEST_URI} [L,R=301]

ErrorDocument 404 /404.html

# ---------- mime ----------
AddType application/javascript .js
AddType image/svg+xml .svg
AddType font/woff2 .woff2
# Without this the manifest is served as octet-stream and Chrome refuses it,
# so the installed-shortcut name and icon fall back to the page title.
AddType application/manifest+json .webmanifest
